  "text": "Three quick ones to close. Question eleven. How do you control warehouse cost in practice? Scan less: prune, name columns, store expensive repeated logic as tables rather than views, and build big append-only tables incrementally. Compute less: right-size, suspend when idle, and keep ad hoc queries off the production warehouse, so they can't starve the pipelines. Question twelve. What is a result cache, and how does it help? It returns a stored result for the exact same query over unchanged data: instantly, and often for free. So it rewards queries that don't vary, and any change to an underlying table invalidates it. Question thirteen. What are distribution and sort keys in Redshift? A distribution key decides which node holds each row: distribute on the join key, and matching rows meet without a shuffle across the network. A sort key orders rows on disk, so range filters skip blocks. Pick a key one value dominates, and one node does most of the work: skew. Every lever here makes a model cheaper. None of them makes it right, and a cheap model that's wrong costs more than any scan. Next: testing and data quality.",
